WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:02.060
Christine Conradt: It’s that obsessing thought,
it truly is.

00:00:02.060 --> 00:00:06.130
I’ve seen so many writers who will sit there
and go over the same thing over and over and

00:00:06.130 --> 00:00:08.709
it’s just not productive at all.

00:00:08.709 --> 00:00:13.969
Film Courage: Right, okay so you’ve seen…I
wanted to talk about once the draft is done.

00:00:13.969 --> 00:00:19.960
I think we’ve heard from a few writers that
some of them do like 3 to 5 pages a day.

00:00:19.960 --> 00:00:25.270
So forgive me, because I’m not in that space
writing scripts but how are you able to do

00:00:25.270 --> 00:00:26.830
15 a day?

00:00:26.830 --> 00:00:27.830
I mean that’s fantastic.

00:00:27.830 --> 00:00:31.990
Christine Conradt: Well, first of all I think
over time you just get better, right?

00:00:31.990 --> 00:00:36.399
So it’s that 10,000 hours, once you put
that in and you become the expert in whatever

00:00:36.399 --> 00:00:39.489
you’re doing, you can recognize when things
aren’t working, you can recognize when they

00:00:39.489 --> 00:00:42.000
are working.

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:49.930
For me to do the 15 pages, you can’t waste
time at all on obsessing over if you like

00:00:49.930 --> 00:00:52.269
this scene or if you like this line of dialogue.

00:00:52.269 --> 00:00:54.930
It is, this is it, it’s on the page and
you move on.

00:00:54.930 --> 00:00:58.160
And then everything is about coming back and
refining it later.

00:00:58.160 --> 00:01:04.150
But getting it out is a much more productive
use of time than getting stuck in sort of

00:01:04.150 --> 00:01:09.400
that turmoil of not knowing if this is good
or if you want to go this direction or whatever.

00:01:09.400 --> 00:01:12.990
You have to make a decision and move forward.

00:01:12.990 --> 00:01:18.009
And when you do that you tend to get a draft
done faster which gives you more time to go

00:01:18.009 --> 00:01:21.600
back and kind of remold that draft.

00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:24.740
A lot of writers also turn in their first
drafts.

00:01:24.740 --> 00:01:30.049
And I think it’s really important to never
show anyone the first draft, to always go

00:01:30.049 --> 00:01:34.870
back at least once, and clean things up because
it makes such a difference.

00:01:34.870 --> 00:01:38.790
Film Courage: Well, yeah…I was curious what
happens after the four weeks?

00:01:38.790 --> 00:01:44.900
You haven’t read it, let’s suppose you
have a 110 pages or so (give or take), I’m

00:01:44.900 --> 00:01:47.270
assuming then that you’re combing through
it?

00:01:47.270 --> 00:01:49.840
Christine Conradt: Well, the 110 would be
in 3 weeks.

00:01:49.840 --> 00:01:53.250
So I give myself that last week to take a
couple of days off, right?

00:01:53.250 --> 00:01:57.270
So at 15 pages a day what is that for a 100
pages?

00:01:57.270 --> 00:02:02.130
I mean you only really need 10 days to write
the initial draft and then the rest of that

00:02:02.130 --> 00:02:07.360
is going back and cleaning it up and moving
your act breaks and tweaking dialogue and

00:02:07.360 --> 00:02:08.360
that kind of stuff.

00:02:08.360 --> 00:02:11.710
Film Courage: Oh…and then you throw in the
3 days to let it breathe for a little bit

00:02:11.710 --> 00:02:12.850
so you give it some space.

00:02:12.850 --> 00:02:14.580
Then you come back and BAM!

00:02:14.580 --> 00:02:17.250
It’s 4 weeks and you have a meeting with
someone.

00:02:17.250 --> 00:02:18.250
That’s how it works?

00:02:18.250 --> 00:02:19.250
Christine Conradt: Yes.

00:02:19.250 --> 00:02:20.250
Film Courage: Oh okay, interesting.

00:02:20.250 --> 00:02:26.200
So at 4 weeks you are showing it to this person
and what is that process like?

00:02:26.200 --> 00:02:30.680
Christine Conradt: So I send it in and it
depends on usually if it’s a very first

00:02:30.680 --> 00:02:32.280
draft, the producer is going to read it first.

00:02:32.280 --> 00:02:36.540
They need to make sure that they’re on board
and when you go to the studio or the network

00:02:36.540 --> 00:02:39.690
then, you go as a team with the producer.

00:02:39.690 --> 00:02:44.150
So they are happy with the draft so they usually
give you a set of notes first.

00:02:44.150 --> 00:02:49.870
I implement those notes and then once they’re
happy, then it goes to the network or the

00:02:49.870 --> 00:02:53.770
studio and we get their notes back.

00:02:53.770 --> 00:02:58.260
By the time they read it we already know that
the producers have approved it for budget

00:02:58.260 --> 00:03:02.880
and they like it and you’ve kind of got
that person in your corner too, if there is

00:03:02.880 --> 00:03:08.400
something to fight for like if they don’t
agree with (I don’t know) whatever creative

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:13.220
decision you’ve made, the producer is already
on board and understanding why you did that

00:03:13.220 --> 00:03:15.760
and they’re sort of on board with you.

00:03:15.760 --> 00:03:20.680
It makes it a more productive meeting also
to have that then just like a bunch of different

00:03:20.680 --> 00:03:23.400
people giving random different notes.

00:03:23.400 --> 00:03:25.070
Those are the worst and I’ve had that experience,
too.

00:03:25.070 --> 00:03:27.420
But it’s not the best way to do it.

00:03:27.420 --> 00:03:32.560
It’s better to kind of like go through the
process correctly because then the drafts

00:03:32.560 --> 00:03:33.560
get better.

00:03:33.560 --> 00:03:39.690
Film Courage: I’m wondering if we can go
in closer to the day that you do 15 pages.

00:03:39.690 --> 00:03:41.950
What time do you start?

00:03:41.950 --> 00:03:43.560
What is it like doing those 15 pages?

00:03:43.560 --> 00:03:47.030
Christine Conradt: I usually get up early,
so I usually get up around 6:00 a.m. and I

00:03:47.030 --> 00:03:49.590
start writing right away.

00:03:49.590 --> 00:03:56.560
I mean I take a break for lunch when I get
hungry and otherwise I’m really just focused

00:03:56.560 --> 00:04:01.430
on getting those pages done and that’s it.

00:04:01.430 --> 00:04:05.340
I don’t know how to tell you anything different.

00:04:05.340 --> 00:04:07.140
It’s just the day and it’s a lot of sitting
in front of a computer.

00:04:07.140 --> 00:04:10.690
Film Courage: So at 6:00 a.m. you’re already
like [going]?

00:04:10.690 --> 00:04:14.980
Christine Conradt: I get more done in the
first 2 hours of my day than probably the

00:04:14.980 --> 00:04:20.410
last 5 hours of my day so whatever I am doing,
if I’m at the gym, it’s a better workout

00:04:20.410 --> 00:04:26.280
in the morning, if I’m here working, I’m
more productive (I’m getting more written).

00:04:26.280 --> 00:04:28.460
So I try to take advantage of those hours.

00:04:28.460 --> 00:04:29.820
I just happen to be a morning person.

00:04:29.820 --> 00:04:31.900
Film Courage: Right, no…I get that.

00:04:31.900 --> 00:04:33.020
We’ve talked to other people about this.

00:04:33.020 --> 00:04:38.480
I think you have less distractions then whether
it’s just knowing you are up before a lot

00:04:38.480 --> 00:04:39.480
of other people or whatever it is.

00:04:39.480 --> 00:04:40.720
Christine Conradt: And it’s controlling
distractions.

00:04:40.720 --> 00:04:45.560
I mean you can check email every 10 minutes
if you want, or check Facebook every 10 minutes

00:04:45.560 --> 00:04:50.440
if you want, but you have to force yourself
not to do that because that really interrupts

00:04:50.440 --> 00:04:52.010
your ability to focus.

00:04:52.010 --> 00:04:56.510
Film Courage: And you’re forcing yourself
not to go back and reread, right?

00:04:56.510 --> 00:04:58.580
Because then you’re going to be stuck on
a scene?

00:04:58.580 --> 00:04:59.660
Christine Conradt: Exactly.

00:04:59.660 --> 00:05:01.850
Film Courage: Now was that always the way
for you?

00:05:01.850 --> 00:05:04.560
Did you go back and reread stuff and did you
learn?

00:05:04.560 --> 00:05:09.730
Christine Conradt: I think I developed that
over needing to be able to finish these projects

00:05:09.730 --> 00:05:12.430
more quickly and find a more efficient way
to do it.

00:05:12.430 --> 00:05:16.160
Because I remember when I was in college and
writing, I would do this thing where I would

00:05:16.160 --> 00:05:17.470
get obsessed with the writer’s block.

00:05:17.470 --> 00:05:18.830
“Oh, I can’t get through this scene.

00:05:18.830 --> 00:05:20.680
This is holding me up.”

00:05:20.680 --> 00:05:25.140
And it’s just a waste and that’s fine
when you’re writing as a hobby.

00:05:25.140 --> 00:05:30.130
It’s not really fine then either, but you
know at least you can manage it.

00:05:30.130 --> 00:05:34.500
When you have deadlines and you’re getting
paid and people are expecting scripts and

00:05:34.500 --> 00:05:38.390
there’s a crew waiting on you so they can
shoot it.

00:05:38.390 --> 00:05:42.210
Yeah, you don’t have that luxury anymore,
so you just find the most efficient way.

